{"title":"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Grouping_Numbers)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Grouping Numbers)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Call finite sets of integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> <i>cousins</i> if \n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> have the same number of elements,</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> are disjoint, and</li>\n<li>the elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> can be paired with the elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> so that the elements in each pair differ by exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/c/5fc355cc4395e0d6bbc6d0acca04c741f90611ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,5\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/e/60e627b38921f04b27c9ce858796953c1010da4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{0,3,4\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" /> are cousins. Suppose that the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> has exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04c607a4d1aa863d2759c3e4c2e7950bffc63cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4040$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> cousins. Find the least number of elements the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> can have.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Grouping_Numbers.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Grouping_Numbers)\">Solution 1 (Grouping Numbers)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Grouping Numbers)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Firstly, notice that if we have an element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, there will be exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> options for the element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> that can pair with the element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. However, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> elements in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> have a difference of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, then their pairs will not be independent. \n</p><p>For example, consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> can pair with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> can pair with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cannot pair with both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possibilities. \n</p><p>As a result, we can show that if we have a group of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> elements in set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> that form an arithmetic sequence with common difference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, then there are exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/cccb103143393c0119f5ab61d6d53a852ce70fcf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> ways to create pairs with elements in set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. This result can be proved by induction. We can model this idea by thinking about separating each \"set\" of number that differ with each neighbor by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and each set separated by a difference of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Then we can easily control what factors we need to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04c607a4d1aa863d2759c3e4c2e7950bffc63cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4040$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/6/696e79a82fd1d5291baa2013cb332ab14f998dcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4040 = 101 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"142\" height=\"17\" />, so our groups of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> elements will result in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/2/6b2237188ed8833c0974332d37c2c3c0cf2b0c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+1 = 101, 2, 2, 2, 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"164\" height=\"17\" />. Thus, the lengths of our groups are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/d/5cd4cc49ce7c8f98cc43d7945ca09b48ba2638a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100, 1, 1, 1, 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>This gives a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/1/201425b8b81c5932567ff99504e19dee29e7150a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 107$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"206\" height=\"15\" /> elements.\n</p><p>Therefore, the least number of elements that set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> can have is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/d/ddd0e26f9e43847324e2f220ea794613a29f472c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{107}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>~pl246631\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/d/c8d49c46ec7684eab24f44d4a130f6a12e080f69.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{array}{ccccccccccc} 1 &amp; &amp; 3 &amp; &amp; 5 &amp; &amp; 7 &amp; &amp; 9 &amp; &amp; \\dots \\\\ / \\ \\backslash &amp; &amp; / \\ \\backslash &amp; &amp; / \\ \\backslash &amp; &amp; / \\ \\backslash &amp; &amp; / \\ \\backslash &amp; &amp; \\\\ 0 \\quad 2 &amp; &amp; 2 \\quad 4 &amp; &amp; 4 \\quad 6 &amp; &amp; 6 \\quad 8 &amp; &amp; 8 \\quad 10 &amp; &amp; \\dots \\end{array}\\]\" width=\"368\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>If two numbers have a difference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e21d790d8618e11200feefc547c07aa98da7119.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d \\neq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"18\" />, then each number has two choices for its cousin set. That means for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> elements, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> choices.\n</p><p>If two numbers have a difference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/d/fbd68fc9b0e901c556adb5c363357d3afbf1b4ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d = 2$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" />, then each number is dependent on the previous number. For example, if the smallest number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/3/7b3cd440c21573dd443ece87cf5c7ce895a21b59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\in n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" /> is chosen, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> has two choices. Either, it chooses the lesser element, in which now the option moves to the next smallest number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/4/4d464921ad89b0c5fe3f1f302dfb61ebe050a150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> options here, or it chooses the greater element, in which now every other option is uniquely determined, so 1 case. That is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> cases.\n</p><p>We require the least number of elements. That implies we must minimize the number of choices we have for each element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. Obviously, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/a/11a2b50f87ea6933bcfe3264fdb8cf5fb1465284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n \\ge n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"86\" height=\"17\" /> for all nonnegative <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, so we require all elements in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> to be of a common difference 2. Moreover, the total number of cousin sets occurs when all possible sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> are constructed from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, and a unique set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> of minimality can be chosen so that in grand account, we obtain 4040 sets.\n</p><p>In other terms, we can combine all possible sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> to obtain 4040 unique sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, rather than have multiple sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> that correspond to multiple sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, all of which adds to 4040. \n</p><p>This bijection is quite easy to solve. Here, we have a total <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/6/696e79a82fd1d5291baa2013cb332ab14f998dcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4040 = 101 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"142\" height=\"17\" /> cousin sets, out of which we have 101, 2, 2, 2, and 5 elements in each unique cousin set. So, in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, we have 100, 1, 1, 1, and 4 elements for each cousin set. When we combine all of these into 1 set, we preserve the fact that we have 4040 cousin sets, as we can simply take the subsets of 100, 1, 1, 1, and 4 of these elements to obtain the requested separated sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Therefore, at minimum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/f/66fd3fa84341d5baafcf2093546f41aea09e7043.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100+1+1+1+4 = \\boxed{107}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"217\" height=\"25\" /> elements.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II\" title=\"2026 AIME II\">2026 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2026 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2026\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wi